From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Echostorm <echostorm@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:14:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Device loss is not fatal: report it through the error sink

Operations that meet a lost device (driver reset/TDR, GPU removed, wgpuDeviceDestroy) panicked
through handle_error_fatal, and a panic in an extern "C" function aborts the process:
wgpuQueueSubmit, wgpuQueueSubmitForIndex, wgpuSurfaceGetCurrentTexture, wgpuSurfaceConfigure,
wgpuDevicePoll, wgpuBufferGet(Const)MappedRange, wgpuRenderBundleEncoderFinish, releasing an
unpresented surface texture (wgpuTextureRelease) and releasing a lost device (wgpuDeviceRelease).
They now report through the device's error sink, as wgpu's own wgpu_core backend does.

- handle_error classifies by wgpu-core's WebGpuError (the source walk missed a DeviceError
  wrapped #[error(transparent)], e.g. QueueSubmitError::Queue, so a lost device's submit
  would have been reported as a validation error).
- wgpu-core's device-lost closure forwards spontaneous losses with their reason; the callback
  runs at most once per device, never after the device is released, and a lost device reports
  no further errors (WebGPU), so none reach the default (fatal) uncaptured-error handler.
- wgpuSurfaceGetCurrentTexture reports status Error with no texture; wgpuSurfacePresent also
  reports to the error sink; the mapped-range getters return NULL; wgpuDevicePoll reports the
  queue empty.
---
 src/conv.rs |   6 ++
 src/lib.rs  | 220 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 2 files changed, 194 insertions(+), 32 deletions(-)

